I'm a reasonable man, get off my case - Radiohead, "Packt Like Sardines In a Crushd Tin Box"

`packt` is a tool for interacting with [blocks] files. 

[blocks]: https://github.com/htogta/blocks

Build it with `just build`, or, if you don't have [just] installed:

```
mkdir -p build && cc -o build/packt src/main.c src/commands.c
```

The binary can then be found at `build/packt`.

`packt` supports the following subcommands:

```
packt help
packt version
packt new blockfile cap
packt info blockfile [-s|--states]
packt clear blockfile [-f|--force] [i[:j]]
packt read blockfile [i[:j]] [file]
packt write blockfile [-e|--error] [-q|--quiet] [i[:j]] [file]
packt print blockfile [-t|--text] [i[:j]]
```

Printing help/usage info:

```
packt help
```

Printing version info for `packt` and its `blocks.h` library dependency:

```
packt version
```

To create a new blockfile, do the following:

```
packt new path_to_file.blocks capacity
```

`capacity` is a 32-bit unsigned integer, which must be at least 1. This capacity *cannot* be resized.

To get info about an existing blockfile, do the following:

```
packt info path_to_file.blocks
```

This prints capacity information- the number of blocks, and the amount of bytes (i.e. number of blocks times 1024). Use the `-s` or `--states` flag to print basic information about the state of each of the blocks (basically just showing you sparse or full sections of the file).

To wipe blocks clean (i.e. overwrite their contents with zeroes), do the following:

```
packt clear path_to_file.blocks i:j
```

Here, `i` and `j` are both block numbers. Doing `1:3` clears blocks 1, 2, and 3. Just doing `1` only clears block 1. Passing *no* number clears the *entire* blockfile. This command prompts you to check if you're sure before performing the operation- you can bypass this prompt with the `-f` or `--force` flag.

To read data from a blockfile, do the following:

```
packt read path_to_file.blocks i:j output_file
```

The `i` and `j` (range) arguments follow the same rules as `packt clear`. If `output_file` is provided, it writes the block data to a file at that path, otherwise it writes the data to standard output. It reads *all* the data from each block- including null bytes that may be at the end of a block.

To write data to a blockfile, do the following:

```
packt write path_to_file.blocks i:j input_file
```

The `i:j` argument behaves the same as it does for `read` and `clear`. If `input_file` is provided, the data that gets written to the blockfile is read from a file at that path, otherwise it writes data coming from standard input. As the data is written, if `packt` runs out of data to write before reaching the end of the block range, *null bytes* are written until it reaches the end of the range, rather than just halting writing. `packt write`, by default, will print a warning if you end up overwriting a block with data in it- you can disable this warning with `-q` or `--quiet`, or, instead, if you'd like it to stop writing and print an error when this occurs instead, use `-e` or `--error`.

Finally, to pretty-print block data, do the following:

```
packt print path_to_file.blocks i:j
```

The range `i:j` behaves the same as it does for the other commands. By default, it prints in "raw" mode- printing the raw hex values for the bytes in each block. To instead print data as text (with line numbers), use the `-t` or `--text` option. 
